<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Internal test-support library (fakes, fixtures, and the port contract-suite
    base once ports exist in F3). Referenced only by test projects; not shipped
    yet, so IsPackable=false. Earns a package when external provider authors need
    the contract suite (spec §1.1, package-boundary.md). TFM/Nullable/etc. are
    inherited from Directory.Build.props — do not re-declare here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The schema-migrator contract-test base (SchemaMigratorContractTests) is a
    public abstract xUnit test class inherited by every provider's own test
    project (Tasks 11-12), so this library needs the test framework and
    assertion library directly — it is not itself covered by
    test/Directory.Build.props, which only applies to projects under test/.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../MMLib.Alvo.Abstractions/MMLib.Alvo.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Abstractions-only, and that is a constraint rather than a coincidence. This library earns a
    package when EXTERNAL PROVIDER AUTHORS need the contract suites, so whatever it references,
    every one of those authors inherits — including one whose store is not EF-backed at all
    (§0's provider model is precisely about not making a consumer adopt one infrastructure
    choice). It also reaches further than it looks: test/Directory.Build.props references this
    project from EVERY test project, so a dependency added here lands in projects that
    deliberately have none — MMLib.Alvo.Abstractions.Tests, MMLib.Alvo.Schema.Tests.

    So a contract suite for a port that does NOT live in Abstractions does not belong here.
    IAlvoSqlDialect is the case in point: statement shape is a relational concern, the port lives
    in MMLib.Alvo.Data.EntityFrameworkCore, and its contract suite plus its T-SQL fake live in the
    companion project MMLib.Alvo.Testing.EntityFrameworkCore — in this library's own namespace, so
    a consumer who adds that package finds them beside the data suites and nothing they already
    wrote moves. The boundary is asserted by
    EfDependencyBoundaryTests.The_shared_test_support_library_does_not_reach_ef_core, because the
    family-wide arch fact matches EF's types by NAME and would never notice.
  -->

  <!--
    InMemoryAlvoData (Task 12) enforces the resolved policy predicate per row through
    IPredicateEvaluator (MMLib.Alvo.Abstractions/Expressions) — a published port, not an
    InternalsVisibleTo into the core. This project stays Abstractions-only: a prior revision
    referenced MMLib.Alvo directly (via InternalsVisibleTo) to reach CelInterpreter, which review
    correctly rejected — MMLib.Alvo.Testing is a package planned to ship, and an unsigned
    InternalsVisibleTo to a published assembly name is forgeable by anyone naming their own
    assembly the same. IPredicateEvaluator is the one real CEL evaluator, reached through a
    contract instead, exactly like IPredicateRenderer is for SQL.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3.extensibility.core" />
    <PackageReference Include="Shouldly" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    SchemaSqlSnapshotTests (the shared generated-SQL snapshot base) verifies
    against Verify baselines, so this library needs Verify directly — the
    project directory / class-name path derivation still comes from each
    consuming test project's own VerifyModuleInit (test/_shared).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Verify.XunitV3" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
